.TH CWKEYER 1 "2026" "ham-cwkeyer" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cwkeyer \- low-latency iambic CW sidetone keyer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cwkeyer
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ device ]
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-setup
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-list\-inputs
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-list\-audio
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-help
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cwkeyer
reads a keyboard-emulating paddle adapter and generates a sidetone. It
implements the Curtis 8044 type B (Accu-keyer) iambic keyer,
with dot/dash paddle memory, and can optionally decode the keyed text.
.B \-\-straight
mode passes the paddles through as a manual key instead.
.PP
The details of reading the paddles and playing the tone belong to the
platform: on Linux the adapter is read through evdev and the sidetone goes to
ALSA, on macOS it is read through IOKit HID and played through CoreAudio (see
.BR MACOS ).
.PP
The paddle inputs are filtered with a digital approximation of the 8044's
external RC network. The recommended 1 M\(*W/0.01 \(*mF network has a time
constant of about 10 ms.
.SH MACOS
The same binary runs on macOS, where a paddle adapter is a plain USB HID
keyboard and no driver or kernel extension is involved. The device argument
is then
.I vendor:product:location
(for example
.IR 04d8:01a6:00110000 )
as printed by
.BR \-\-list\-inputs .
The last field is the USB port, so it changes when the adapter is moved; the
argument may stop at a field boundary, and
.I 04d8:01a6
selects the adapter in any port as long as that is unambiguous. The built-in
keyboard reports zeros for vendor and product, so it needs the full id, and it
is usable as a paddle with
.B \-\-no\-grab
and your own key names.
.PP
Reading keyboard input needs the Input Monitoring permission, which macOS
attributes to the terminal that launched the keyer. If the permission has
been denied, the keyer reports it at startup and from the setup wizard rather
than waiting for a tap that never arrives. Taking exclusive control stands in
for evdev's grab and is refused for keyboards unless the process is root, so
the keyer avoids it when it is not possible, and the setup wizard offers the
same default.
.B \-\-grab
forces an attempt; one that fails warns and falls back. Without exclusive
access the levers also reach the foreground application. The
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
and
.B PULSE_SINK
variables do not apply; use
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
to choose the output by name or UID. Latency is the output device's buffer,
and the achieved value is printed at startup and at exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I device
The input device to read. On Linux this is an evdev node such as
.IR /dev/input/event3 ;
on macOS it is
.I vendor:product:location
as printed by
.BR \-\-list\-inputs .
The same value can be given with
.BR \-\-device .
At most one positional argument is accepted, and there are no positional
speed, frequency, or key-mapping arguments.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Actions
.TP
.BR \-\-setup ", " \-s
Interactively choose the paddle device by tapping the dot lever and then the
dash lever, then run with the settings given on the command line (or the
defaults). The device and both key codes are learned from the taps, so no
.I device
is needed and giving one is an error. If exactly one known paddle adapter is
present, the tap test is skipped and the adapter's table default keys are
used. If both taps use the same key, it offers straight-key mode. Requires an
interactive terminal on standard input.
.TP
.BR \-\-list\-inputs ", " \-L
List the readable input devices, grouped as
.BR unknown ,
.BR keyboard ,
or
.BR paddle .
Groups are ordered so paddles come last, nearest the shell prompt, and the
devices in each group are sorted by event number. A device that reports the
default LEFTCTRL/RIGHTCTRL paddle keys is called out. The category names are
colored only when standard output is a terminal; set
.B NO_COLOR
to disable them. The
.B keyboard
or
.B paddle
guess is a hint only and is not used to select a device.
.TP
.BR \-\-list\-audio ", " \-A
List the audio devices the platform accepts, with the names its audio
settings use. On Linux these are ALSA PCM names for
.BR CW_ALSA_DEVICE ,
with a reminder that PulseAudio/PipeWire sinks are selected through
.BR PULSE_SINK .
On macOS these are the names and UIDs, with the nominal rate, current buffer,
and system defaults, for
.BR CW_AUDIO_DEVICE ,
.BR CW_MIX_MIC_DEVICE ,
and
.BR CW_MIX_MONITOR .
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit.
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-\-device " DEVICE"
The input device. Equivalent to the single positional
.IR device .
.TP
.B \-\-grab
Request exclusive access even if it may fail. Without this, the keyer takes
exclusive access when the backend says it can.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-grab
Never take exclusive access; useful when testing on the keyboard you are
typing on.
.SS Keying
.TP
.BI \-\-wpm " WPM"
Keying speed in words per minute, 1\(en40 (default 12). At 40 WPM the full
\(+-25 ms weighting range still leaves positive marks and spaces.
.TP
.BI \-\-freq " HZ"
Sidetone frequency in hertz (default 800).
.TP
.BI \-\-dit " KEY"
.TQ
.BI \-\-dah " KEY"
Key names or codes for the dit and dah paddles (default
.B LEFTCTRL
and
.BR RIGHTCTRL ).
Known names include
.BR COMMA ,
.BR DOT ,
.BR LEFT ,
.BR RIGHT ,
.BR Z ,
and
.BR X ;
a
.B KEY_
prefix and raw numeric codes are also accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-straight
Use the paddles as a straight key: either lever (or both, squeezed) keys the
sidetone directly, with no iambic timing or memory. The RC paddle filter still
applies. This is the software equivalent of the 8044ABM's separate
.B MAN. KEY
input.
.B \-\-decode
cannot be combined with this option.
.TP
.B \-\-decode
Print each keyed symbol decoded to text, without newlines. Unknown symbols are
ignored, and a pause longer than seven dits prints a space.
.TP
.BI \-\-level " PERCENT"
Sidetone level as a percentage of full scale, 0\(en100 (default 70 or
.BR CW_KEYER_LEVEL ).
.TP
.BI \-\-weight " MS"
Weighting in milliseconds, \(en25\(en25 (default 0 or
.BR CW_KEYER_WEIGHT_MS ).
A constant time is added to every mark and removed from the following space,
so the element period and character speed are unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-debounce " MS"
Paddle RC filter time constant in milliseconds, 0\(en50 (default 10 or
.BR CW_KEYER_DEBOUNCE_MS ).
Set to 0 to disable the filter.
.SS Audio
.TP
.B \-\-mix\-mic
Mix the microphone into the sidetone, so that one output device carries both
and a voice application can take the operator and the sidetone from a single
input. macOS only; use
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
and
.B CW_MIX_MIC_DEVICE
to say where the mix goes and which microphone to capture, and
.B CW_MIX_MONITOR
to hear the sidetone locally. On Linux
.BR cw-mic-mix (1)
does the same with PulseAudio.
.PP
Values may be written as
.BI \-\-wpm " 25"
or
.BR \-\-wpm=25 ;
short options may be
.BI \-w " 25"
or
.BR \-w25 .
.B \-\-
ends option parsing. Command-line options override the environment, and the
environment overrides the built-in defaults. Usage errors exit 2; runtime
failures exit 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Command-line options override these variables; these variables override the
built-in defaults. The sidetone settings apply everywhere; the audio output
variables are per platform.
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
Linux only. ALSA PCM to open (default
.BR pulse ).
.B cwkeyer \-\-list\-audio
prints the PCM names.
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_LATENCY_US
Linux only. Requested ALSA latency in microseconds (default 20000). ALSA
may negotiate a different buffer and period; the achieved values are printed
at startup.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_LEVEL
Sidetone level as a percentage of full scale, 0\(en100 (default 70). Can also
be set with
.BR \-\-level .
The level can be changed while running with
.B 9
and
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_DEBOUNCE_MS
Paddle RC filter time constant in milliseconds, 0\(en50 (default 10). Can
also be set with
.BR \-\-debounce .
The 8044's recommended 1 M\(*W/0.01 \(*mF network is about 10 ms. Set to 0
to disable the filter; a shorter value gives a snappier response at high
speed.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_WEIGHT_MS
Weighting in milliseconds, \(en25\(en25 (default 0, strict ITU timing). Can
also be set with
.BR \-\-weight .
A constant time is added to every mark and removed from the space that
follows it, so the element period and the character speed are unchanged.
This matches the 8044ABM's RC weight control, which stretches elements by
a fixed amount rather than a fixed ratio, so its relative effect depends on
the keying speed. Can also be changed while running with
.B ,
and
.BR . .
.TP
.B PULSE_SINK
Linux only. PulseAudio/PipeWire sink for the sidetone. If
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
is
.B pulse
and this is unset or empty, the keyer tries
.BR cw_out ,
the combined sink created by
.BR cw-mic-mix (1).
If that sink is unavailable it falls back to the system default sink with a
warning, so a bare run still plays.
.TP
.B CW_MIX_MONITOR
macOS only. Output device on which to play the sidetone alone, so the operator
can hear what they are sending while the mixed stream goes to a voice
application. Independent of
.BR \-\-mix\-mic .
Runs about one device buffer behind the output.
.TP
.B CW_MIX_MIC_DEVICE
macOS only, with
.BR \-\-mix\-mic .
Input device by name or UID to capture (default: the system input).
Capturing needs the Microphone permission, which macOS attributes to the
program that launched the keyer.
.TP
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
macOS only. Output device by name or UID, which pins it. The default
.RB ( default )
tracks the system output, including later changes, so headphones plugged in
mid-session take the sidetone with them.
.B cwkeyer \-\-list\-audio
prints the names and UIDs.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, do not color the output of
.B \-\-list\-inputs
or the setup wizard.
.SH RUNTIME CONTROLS
If standard input is a readable terminal or pipe,
.B cwkeyer
accepts these single-key controls while it is running:
.TP
.B [
and
.B ]
Slower and faster, 1 WPM per step (limited to 1\(en40 WPM).
.TP
.B -
and
.B =
(or
.BR + )
Lower and higher pitch, 10 Hz per step.
.TP
.B 9
and
.B 0
Quieter and louder, 5 percent per step.
.TP
.B ,
and
.B .
Lighter and heavier weighting, 1 ms per step.
.PP
Pending keys in a pipe are consumed before the keyer treats its closed
writer as end-of-input. Changes take effect on the next element. On a
terminal the current settings are shown on a single status line that is
rewritten in place rather than appended line by line. The controls are read from
standard input, not from the paddle device, so avoid mapping a paddle
to one of these keys.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
stop the keyer and restore the terminal and the input grab.
.B SIGHUP
and
.B SIGQUIT
do the same.
.B SIGTSTP
(Ctrl+Z) releases the terminal and the input device before suspending and
re-establishes them on
.BR SIGCONT .
Ordinary signal cleanup is performed by a dedicated thread, not in an
asynchronous signal handler. Terminal restoration after a fatal crash
cannot be guaranteed; use
.B stty sane
if necessary.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
When the keyer stops it prints a summary to standard error if any audio
interruptions occurred (an ALSA underrun, or a jump in CoreAudio's sample
timeline) or if any element started more than a millisecond later than its
nominal mark plus space, together with the worst delay. A run with no audio
problems prints nothing there, except that on macOS the measured output
latency is always reported.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3
12 WPM, 800 Hz on a keyboard, with exclusive access when possible.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3 --wpm 25 --freq 700
25 WPM at 700 Hz.
.TP
.B cwkeyer --device /dev/input/event3 --dit COMMA --dah DOT --no-grab --decode
Remap the paddles, do not grab the device, and decode to text.
.TP
.B cwkeyer --setup --wpm 25 --freq 700
Choose the device by tapping, then start at 25 WPM, 700 Hz.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/eventX --straight
Use the paddles as a straight key instead of an iambic keyer.
.TP
.B cwkeyer --list-inputs
List the readable paddle and keyboard devices.
.TP
.B cwkeyer --list-audio
List the audio devices and their names or UIDs.
.SH FILES
The files below are Linux; on macOS the paddle is a HID device with no node
(see
.BR MACOS ).
.TP
.I /dev/input/eventX
Paddle input device.
.TP
.I /dev/input/
evdev devices; see the README for the recommended udev rule.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evtest (8),
.BR libinput (1),
.BR pactl (1),
.BR cw-mic-mix (1)
and the project README, on Linux.
.PP
On macOS the equivalents are IORegistryExplorer or
.B ioreg (8)
for HID devices, Audio MIDI Setup for audio devices, and the BlackHole driver
for the microphone mix.
